From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 03:26:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] win32u, winex11: notice a display change the notification
 missed.

The configuration every process reads is a copy, refreshed when the driver
says the host changed.  That works while the notification arrives.  When it
does not, nothing goes back to check: the copy goes on describing a screen that
is gone, every process sizes its windows from it, and none of them can be
resized past it -- which from outside is not a display problem anyone
recognises as one.  Windows has no such state to be in.  There the
configuration is the live one, and a program that asks gets what is true now.

Three things kept the notification from arriving.

RandR has two event types, RRScreenChangeNotify and RRNotify; crtc, output and
provider changes are subtypes carried inside the second, not event numbers of
their own.  Registering by subtype put one handler on RRScreenChangeNotify by
accident, one on RRNotify, and one on an event the extension does not have.
The screen change was never asked for either, so a host that resizes the screen
without reconfiguring a crtc -- a remote desktop whose viewport moved, a nested
server whose window was resized -- reported it in the one form nobody was
listening for.

Publishing the new configuration is the desktop thread's job, and keeping it to
one thread is what stops every process enumerating at once.  But the event
reaches every process that selected for it and the desktop thread is not always
among them; when it was not, the handler returned having done nothing.  It now
publishes from any thread that finds what win32u reports no longer matching the
host, and the first one through fixes it for the rest.

That publish cannot use a synchronous send: it waits for the desktop thread to
answer, and a desktop thread waiting on this one never will.  From anywhere but
the desktop thread the notification is posted instead.

And a floor under all of it: the driver gains pGetHostDisplayKey, something
cheap that moves when the host's display does -- the root window geometry on
X11, one round trip and no enumeration.  win32u takes it beside the
configuration it belongs to and compares before serving that configuration
again, rate limited because this runs in front of every display query,
GetSystemMetrics included.  A stale configuration can still be served between
checks; what it cannot do any more is last for the life of the session.

Measured against a nested server whose screen is resized under a running
session, with a wineserver session held open across the change: the build
before this follows the change and so does the build after, so nothing here
regresses what already worked.  The failure this was written for -- a remote
desktop resized from the client, where the configuration stayed behind for
good -- has not been reproduced in a harness, and the causal chain from these
three to that failure is not yet proven.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit d2fb8bc4ad8a697803fecbac23a90dab6e1317bb)

